D(3,M,"house-of-hundreds-2-adding-three-digit-numbers",["three-digit","palindrome","twin digits","number line"],
 ("Birbal counts the crows","Akbar wanted to know how many crows were in the city. Birbal said, “Exactly 963.” “What if there are fewer?” “Some have gone on holiday.” “What if more?” “Visitors have come.” Akbar was pleased."),
 ["Welcome to House of Hundreds Two. Akbar and Birbal were walking in a garden watching crows. Birbal said there were 963 crows in the city, and cleverly explained any difference.",
  "Three-digit numbers are made of hundreds, tens and ones. Draw tiles to show 832, 947, 726, 504, 620 and 700.",
  "Locate numbers on a number line. 530 and 540 are close, 628 lies between 620 and 660, and 696 is just before 700.",
  "The Number Detective game has special numbers. 100, 200, 300 are hundreds. 123, 345 and 789 have digits going up. 876 and 321 go down.",
  "Numbers like 11, 22, 33 have twin digits and 111, 222, 333 have triplet digits.",
  "Numbers like 353 and 868 look the same from left to right and from right to left. These are called palindromes (additional, not in the textbook).",
  "Make numbers with paper slips marked 100, 10 and 1. With 6 slips you can make 420 with four 100s and two 10s? Check how many hundreds and tens you need.",
  "To make 231 you need two slips of 100, three of 10 and one of 1."],
 ("Which slips make 231?",[("2 hundreds = 2 slips of 100.","Hundreds first."),("3 tens = 3 slips of 10.","Then tens."),("1 one = 1 slip of 1.","Ones last. Total 6 slips.")]),
 ("A friend says 353 reads differently backwards. What do you say?",["353 reads the same backwards","It is a special number"],["All numbers do"]),
 ["A three-digit number has hundreds, tens and ones.","Slips of 100, 10 and 1 can build numbers.","Some numbers look the same both ways."],
 [("m","Which slips make 231?","2 hundreds 3 tens 1 one",["3 hundreds 2 tens 1 one","2 hundreds 1 ten 3 ones"],"The digits tell the hundreds, tens and ones in order."),
  ("t","353 reads the same from both sides.",True,"3-5-3 is the same backwards."),
  ("m","Which has triplet digits?","222",["212","220"],"All three digits are the same.")],
 title="Reading and building three-digit numbers")
D(3,M,"house-of-hundreds-2-subtracting-three-digit-numbers",["subtract","take away","regroup","difference"],
 ("How many are left?","A driver carries 700 sacks of rice and delivers 250. How many are left?"),
 ["To subtract, take away the ones, then the tens, then the hundreds.",
  "Think of 700 - 250. 700 is 7 hundreds. Take away 2 hundreds to get 500, then take away 5 tens to get 450.",
  "If there are not enough ones, break one ten into ten ones. This is called regrouping.",
  "The difference is the answer of a subtraction. It tells how much more or how much less.",
  "Use a number line to count back or count up. From 250 count up to 700 to find the difference.",
  "Check subtraction by adding: 450 + 250 should be 700."],
 ("Find 653 - 356.",[("Ones: 3 - 6 not possible. Break one ten to get 13 ones. 13 - 6 = 7.","Regroup."),("Tens: now 4 tens. 4 - 5 not possible. Break one hundred: 14 tens - 5 = 9.","Regroup again."),("Hundreds: 5 - 3 = 2. Answer 297.","Check: 297 + 356 = 653.")]),
 ("How can you check 700 - 250 = 450?",["Add 450 and 250","The total must be 700"],["Subtract again"]),
 ["Subtract ones, tens, hundreds.","Regroup when needed.","Check by adding."],
 [("m","700 - 250 =","450",["550","350"],"Take 2 hundreds and 5 tens."),
  ("t","We can check subtraction by addition.",True,"450 + 250 = 700."),
  ("m","653 - 356 =","297",["307","297 is wrong"],"Regroup twice.")])
D(3,M,"house-of-hundreds-2-adding-with-regrouping-and-estimating",["carry","regroup","estimate","nearest hundred"],
 ("Adding rice sacks","A driver carried 345 sacks one day and 289 the next. How many in all?"),
 ["To add big numbers, add ones, then tens, then hundreds.",
  "When ones make 10 or more, regroup 10 ones as 1 ten and carry it. 5 + 9 = 14, write 4 and carry 1 ten.",
  "Do the same for tens: 4 + 8 + 1 = 13, write 3 and carry 1 hundred. Then hundreds: 3 + 2 + 1 = 6. So 345 + 289 = 634.",
  "Estimating means finding an answer that is close without exact work. Round to the nearest hundred: 345 is about 300 and 289 is about 300, so the answer is about 600.",
  "Compare the estimate with the exact answer to see if it is sensible. 634 is close to 600."],
 ("Add 345 and 289.",[("Ones: 5 + 9 = 14, write 4 carry 1.","Regroup."),("Tens: 4 + 8 + 1 = 13, write 3 carry 1.","Regroup."),("Hundreds: 3 + 2 + 1 = 6. Answer 634.","Join.")]),
 ("Why estimate before adding?",["To check if the answer is sensible","Round to the nearest hundred"],["To avoid adding"]),
 ["Carry 10 ones to make 1 ten.","Estimate by rounding.","Check your answer with the estimate."],
 [("m","345 + 289 =","634",["624","534"],"Carry twice."),
  ("t","345 is nearly 300 to the nearest hundred.",True,"345 is closer to 300 than 400."),
  ("m","10 ones make","1 ten",["1 hundred","1 thousand"],"Ten ones are one ten.")])
D(3,M,"fun-at-class-party-units-of-length",["hand span","footstep","paper string","unit"],
 ("Measuring the door","At the class party, Shelly measures the height of the door with paper strings. Leena and Adi use hand spans to measure the same table. Will they get the same number?"),
 ["The class is preparing for a party. The picture shows many ways to measure length.",
  "Shelly finds the height of the door using paper strings. Others use hand spans, footsteps or a ruler.",
  "Leena and Adi measure the same table with their own hand spans. Their answers may differ, because their hands are different sizes.",
  "That is why we need a standard unit that is the same for everybody, like a centimetre or a metre.",
  "Colour the shortest paper string red and the longest green. Compare strings by laying them side by side.",
  "Hand spans and footsteps are informal units. Rulers and tapes use formal units."],
 ("Why do two children get different hand-span counts?",[("Everybody's hand is a different size.","Sizes differ."),("A bigger hand covers more, so fewer spans.","More length each."),("So we use standard units.","Same for all.")]),
 ("Is a hand span a good unit for selling cloth?",["No, hands differ","Use metres"],["Yes, hands are equal"]),
 ["Length can be measured with hand spans, steps and strings.","Different people get different counts.","We use standard units."],
 [("m","Leena and Adi measure the same table with hand spans. Their counts may be","Different",["Always same","Always 5"],"Their hand sizes are different."),
  ("t","A ruler gives a measure that is the same for everyone.",True,"It uses a standard unit."),
  ("m","The longest string is the one that is","Longest side by side",["Thickest","Reddest"],"Lay them side by side to compare.")])
D(3,M,"fun-at-class-party-metres-and-centimetres",["centimetre","metre","ruler","tape"],
 ("Distance between walls","How far is it between the two walls of the classroom? Children found it with steps, a rope and a metre tape."),
 ["A centimetre (cm) is a small unit. It is about the width of your finger.",
  "A metre (m) is a bigger unit. A metre is a hundred centimetres, so 1 m = 100 cm.",
  "We measure short things like a pencil in centimetres and long things like a room in metres.",
  "To measure with a ruler, place the zero mark at one end of the object and read where the other end falls.",
  "A tape measure is useful for longer lengths like the window or the classroom wall.",
  "Find the distance between two walls. Different ways give different counts, so use a standard measure."],
 ("A pencil is 15 cm long. A rope is 2 m long. Which is longer?",[("2 m = 200 cm.","Change to the same unit."),("200 cm is more than 15 cm.","Compare."),("The rope is longer.","Answer.")]),
 ("Where does the ruler start?",["At the zero mark","Line up the end of the object"],["At 1"]),
 ["1 metre = 100 centimetres.","Use cm for small things and m for long things.","Start measuring at zero."],
 [("m","1 metre is","100 centimetres",["10 centimetres","1000 centimetres"],"A metre is a hundred cm."),
  ("t","A classroom wall is measured in metres.",True,"It is a long length."),
  ("m","Which unit is best for a pencil?","cm",["m","km"],"A pencil is short.")])
